<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Want Five Pineapples. – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Go Shopping!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Want Five Pineapples.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify the main idea from short spoken passages. Interactive: Participate in call-and-response activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify the main idea of short passages about shopping (e.g., "I want five pineapples."). Can participate in call-and-response activities (e.g., call out "Let's go shopping. Walk, walk, walk." Everyone repeats and mimics the action.). Can follow simple instructions at the store (e.g., "Buy three bananas and two oranges.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engage students, activate prior knowledge, and introduce the topic. Model target language and check for understanding.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts with a "Let's Go Shopping!" chant and TPR. Teacher says, "Let's go shopping! Walk, walk, walk!" and mimics walking. Students repeat and mimic. Teacher introduces fruit flashcards (pineapple, apple, orange, banana) one by one, saying the word clearly. Students listen and repeat chorally. Teacher shows number flashcards (one to five, then ten) and counts aloud. Students listen and repeat. The teacher asks, "What do we buy at the store?" and points to fruit flashcards.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the phrase "I want five pineapples." using a flashcard of five pineapples. Teacher holds up flashcards of fruits and numbers and clearly articulates, "I want one apple, please." "I want three bananas, please." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /b/ in "banana" and /p/ in "pineapple" through repetition and exaggeration. Teacher also models blending phonemes for "six" (/s-ɪ-k-s/) and "money" (/ˈm-ʌ-n-i/) by saying the individual sounds then the whole word.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a flashcard of three bananas and asks, "How many bananas do I want?" (Expected answer: "Three.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to an apple flashcard and asks, "Is this an orange or an apple?" (Expected answer: "An apple.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Introduce new language in context through authentic or semi-authentic materials, focusing on listening comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content:</b> The teacher plays a short audio clip or reads a simple dialogue between a customer and a cashier at a store. The dialogue includes target vocabulary (pineapple, apple, orange, banana, money, buy, cost) and grammar (number + noun, "I want...", "How much?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of a short, simple shopping dialogue (e.g., "Customer: Hello! I want two apples, please. Cashier: Here you go! Two apples. Customer: And I want one pineapple, please. Cashier: One pineapple. That's three dollars. Customer: Here's the money. Thank you!"). The teacher uses corresponding flashcards or visuals displayed during the audio.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret simple oral messages related to daily life activities, specifically identifying items and quantities in a shopping context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question (1st Listening):</b> Teacher asks, "What are they buying?" Students listen and point to the fruit flashcards they hear (e.g., apple, pineapple).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (2nd Listening):</b> Teacher reads simple statements related to the dialogue. Students listen and show "thumbs up" for True or "thumbs down" for False.
                        <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The customer wants three bananas." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The customer wants one pineapple." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching (3rd Listening):</b> Teacher gives students a simple worksheet with pictures of fruits and numbers. Students listen to the dialogue again and draw a line from the fruit picture to the correct number they heard. (e.g., apple picture to '2', pineapple picture to '1').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10-12 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Controlled and semi-controlled activities to practice accurate listening comprehension and receptive identification of target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content (Listening Accuracy):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Number + noun (e.g., "three bananas"), Present simple ("I want five pineapples."), Numbers (one, two, three, four, five, ten).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> pineapple, apple, orange, banana, money, cart, market, store, basket, price, cashier, buy, cost.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /b/ in "banana", /p/ in "pineapple", blending of /s-ɪ-k-s/ in "six" and /ˈm-ʌ-n-i/ in "money".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic/Sociolinguistic:</b> Expressing quantity: "I want three apples, please." Requesting items: "Can I have two bananas, please?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listen and Circle):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with groups of fruit pictures, each group showing different quantities (e.g., one apple, three apples, five apples). The teacher reads aloud sentences like, "I want three apples." or "Can I have two bananas, please?" Students listen carefully and circle the correct quantity of fruit. This activity explicitly targets number + noun combinations and polite requests.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Call and Response with Numbers):</b> The teacher leads a call-and-response activity. The teacher says a number and a fruit (e.g., "Teacher: Four oranges!"), and students respond with the full sentence, "Students: I want four oranges, please!" The teacher then varies it (e.g., "Teacher: Ten bananas!"), and students respond, "Students: I want ten bananas, please!" The teacher focuses on clear listening and accurate repetition, ensuring correct pronunciation of /b/ and /p/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listen and Pick):</b> Students work in pairs with a set of fruit flashcards and number cards on their desk. One student (the "customer") says, "I want three pineapples, please." The other student (the "store clerk") listens, identifies the item and quantity, and then picks up the correct number card and pineapple flashcard. They then swap roles. The teacher circulates, listening to student interactions and providing feedback on clear listening and correct item identification.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(15-18 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the target language in a realistic, communicative task, focusing on fluency and completing the task in the "Let's Go Shopping!" scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: "My Shopping List" Role-Play</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Students will engage in a simple role-play where they pretend to be customers at a fruit stand. The teacher will act as the "cashier" or select a confident student to do so. Each student receives a "shopping list" (a small picture card showing 1-3 items with quantities, e.g., "2 bananas," "1 pineapple," "3 oranges"). Students take turns approaching the "cashier" and making their requests. The primary focus for the students is to *listen* to the teacher/cashier's confirmation or questions, and for the cashier, to *listen* to the customer's request and respond correctly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher sets up a "fruit stand" with flashcards of pineapples, apples, oranges, and bananas, and optional "money" cutouts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student receives a small picture card acting as their "shopping list" (e.g., a card showing "3 bananas" and "1 apple").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students approach the "fruit stand" one by one. The "customer" says, "Hello! I want [number] [fruit], please." based on their card. (e.g., "I want three bananas, please.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher (as cashier) *listens* to the request and confirms, "Three bananas? Here you go!" or clarifies, "Did you say three or five bananas?" The teacher hands over the corresponding flashcards.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The customer then says "Thank you!" The activity emphasizes active listening from both sides, ensuring the correct items and quantities are identified and exchanged.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(7-8 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment to check learning outcomes and student self-reflection on their comprehension and participation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "My Shopping List" role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the 'Shopping List' role-play, making requests and responding to the cashier. The teacher observes if students can correctly identify the items and quantities when listening to the cashier's confirmation/questions (e.g., "Did you say three bananas?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify the main idea (the item being bought) from spoken requests.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify specific quantities (numbers 1-10) in spoken requests.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participates in call-and-response activities with appropriate phrases.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of target vocabulary (e.g., /b/ in banana, /p/ in pineapple) is intelligible.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a simple paper slip or asked to raise hands.</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can hear the difference between 'one' and 'two' apples." (Yes/No/A little)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I understood when the teacher asked, 'How many bananas?'" (Happy face/Neutral face/Sad face)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students and teacher reflect on the lesson, learning, and future adjustments. Connect to the next lesson in the sequence.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new shopping words did I hear today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to understand when people talked about buying fruit?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening-focused lesson? (e.g., TPR, flashcards, call-and-response engagement).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was challenging for students regarding listening comprehension? (e.g., distinguishing certain numbers, identifying specific fruits, speed of speech).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to reinforce vocabulary and grammar for the upcoming reading tasks? (e.g., more visual cues, slower audio, more repetition of specific phrases).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Great job listening today! Next time, we will look at some pictures and read about shopping to see these words, so you can read what you heard today."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(3-5 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>